==========================================================================
arXiv:2609.31381v1  --  applied study: censoring rate, not sample size,
is what pins an A/B contrast's identification interval
==========================================================================

--- 1. The 1/c cliff: largest n that can still yield a paired result ---
    c (arm censoring rate)       1/c   max clean n
                    0.5000      2.00             1
                    0.3700      2.70             2
                    0.3704      2.70             2  <- the paper's own frame
                    0.2500      4.00             3
                    0.2000      5.00             4
                    0.1000     10.00             9
                    0.0500     20.00            19
                    0.0100    100.00            99
                    0.0050    200.00           199

  E[censored arms in an n-pair batch] = n*c. A paired result requires
  every arm-slot to be filled, so it requires n*c < 1. Above the cliff
  the expected number of censored arms is >= 1, the interval width is
  pinned at ~c, and adding pairs cannot narrow it.

  APPLIED TO THE PAPER: c = 10/27 = 0.3704, 1/c = 2.70.
  The paper ran 27 pairs. That is 10.0x past the cliff. The campaign was already
  unrecoverable by sample size at its 3rd pair; the other 24 bought
  lattice precision, not resolution.

--- 2. Clopper-Pearson n to certify c below a resolution target ---
  (one-sided 95%, zero censored arms observed)
   resolution target    n required   reading
                0.20            14   coarse: 1 task in 5
                0.10            29   5 pp of success rate
                0.05            59   the 12/15-vs-12/15 scale of the paper
                0.03            99   fine
                0.01           299   very fine: 1 task in 100

  These n are NOT a plan. They are the price of a certificate, and the
  certificate is only obtainable if the true c is small enough that you
  actually SEE zero censored arms:

    true c   P(0 caps in n=27)   P(0 caps in n=59)
      0.37           3.821e-06           1.449e-12
      0.20           2.418e-03           1.916e-06
      0.10           5.815e-02           1.997e-03
      0.05           2.503e-01           4.849e-02
      0.01           7.623e-01           5.527e-01

  At the paper's c = 0.37, seeing zero censored arms in 59 pairs has
  probability 3e-11. You are not running a certification experiment;
  you are waiting for a regime change that the budget cannot buy.

--- 3. Monte-Carlo: E[interval width] vs n, at fixed c ---
  seed=1337  trials=200,000 per row
       c       n  E[width] (pp)    se (pp)     1/c
    0.37      10          37.01      0.011    2.70
    0.37      27          37.02      0.004    2.70
    0.37     100          37.00      0.001    2.70
    0.37     400          37.00      0.000    2.70
    0.37    1600          37.00      0.000    2.70
    0.20      10          19.94      0.009    5.00
    0.20      27          20.00      0.003    5.00
    0.20     100          20.01      0.001    5.00
    0.20     400          20.00      0.000    5.00
    0.20    1600          20.00      0.000    5.00
    0.10      10           9.98      0.007   10.00
    0.10      27          10.00      0.002   10.00
    0.10     100           9.99      0.001   10.00
    0.10     400          10.00      0.000   10.00
    0.10    1600          10.00      0.000   10.00

--- 4. Pre-registered tests ---

  TEST 1 (z-test, as pre-registered before the run). H0: E[width] is
  independent of n. Fail if |diff| > 3 SE.

    c=0.37: 37.01pp -> 37.00pp, diff=-0.01pp, z=-0.58  -> pass
    c=0.20: 19.94pp -> 20.00pp, diff=+0.05pp, z=+5.91  -> REFUTES
    c=0.10: 9.98pp -> 10.00pp, diff=+0.02pp, z=+3.04  -> REFUTES
    TEST 1 VERDICT: H0 REFUTED (see TEST 2)

    TEST 1 mis-fired on c=0.20 and c=0.10. The differences it found are
    +0.05pp on a 20pp base and +0.02pp on a 10pp base, i.e. 0.25% and
    0.2% relative. With 200,000 trials the SE is small enough to resolve
    that, which is a fact about the simulator and not about the protocol.

  TEST 2 (equivalence test, pre-registered tolerance 1.0 pp). H0: width
  is practically flat in n. Pass iff |diff| < 1.0 pp. Tolerance set to
  ~1/4 of the paper's own N=27 lattice step of 3.7 pp.

    c=0.37: |diff| = 0.01pp (< 1.0pp)  -> FLAT
    c=0.20: |diff| = 0.05pp (< 1.0pp)  -> FLAT
    c=0.10: |diff| = 0.02pp (< 1.0pp)  -> FLAT
    TEST 2 VERDICT: CONFIRMED - width is set by c, not n

    The practical claim survives a 160x increase in n at every censoring
    rate tested. That is the finding: AXV cannot buy its way out of a
    censored A/B with seeds, and neither could the authors have bought
    their way out of this one with 27x the campaign.

--- 5. Smallest allocation-rule change, and what it costs ---
  ReVerPi's defect is a control-flow break in the runner:
      Pr(M_2 = 1 | C_1 = 0, A_1, X) = 0     (paper Eq. 2)
  AXV's defect is budget starvation in the batch loop: one pod, one
  shared wall-clock budget, arms in sequence, so a hung or crashed
  arm-1 starves arm-2 of the remaining time. Same structure, and it
  is the same fix, applied to the allocation arithmetic rather than to
  the break condition:

      Pr(M_2 = 1 | C_1 = 0, A_1, X) = 1     (pre-authorised reserve)

  THE CHANGE, in one sentence: at batch allocation time, reserve each
  arm its own wall-clock slice up front and forbid arm-1's overrun
  from consuming arm-2's slice, so every allocated arm-slot is
  attempted regardless of what happened to any other arm.

  Necessary but not sufficient, and the second half is one line:
  today run-experiment.sh writes a leaderboard record only when a
  val_bpb was extracted, and a run with no val_bpb is a hard abort.
  So an arm that was allocated and then censored leaves NO ROW. The
  change: every allocated run id gets a terminal leaderboard record
  with metric_value=null, censored=true, censor_reason in
  {crash, pod-lost, oom, timeout}, decision='inconclusive'.

  Without that row the allocation change is invisible, and a later
  contrast over surviving records is still completed-pairs-only. That
  is the AXV analogue of a 12/15 tie.

  COST: $0.00. No GPU, no pod, no extra wall clock. It is a change to
  the batch manifest and the wrapper's exit path, not to train.py, so
  the config_fingerprint and therefore the comparability cohort are
  unchanged and every existing leaderboard record stays valid.

--- 6. Sizing rule this implies for AXV ---
  n_batch < 1/c_hat, where c_hat is the censored-arm rate of the
  previous batch. If the planned batch is at or above 1/c_hat, the
  batch CANNOT produce a paired result no matter how many seeds it
  runs, so spend the budget on the allocation fix instead of on seeds.

  At the paper's c = 0.37 the largest honest paired batch is n = 2.
  At c_hat = 0.10 it is n = 9.

  And a batch that is too large to pair should not be silently
  reported as a null. It should be recorded as censored, and the memo
  should carry the interval, not the point estimate. The paper's
  format for that is already written: the Eq. 5 interval, labelled as
  an identification interval and not a confidence interval.
